{
  "technical_and_design_decisions": [
    "For SetXXX functions operating on maps (e.g., organizations, policies, ACLs, capabilities), adopt pure override semantics - simply replace the existing entry without error.",
    "For SetXXX functions operating on slices (e.g., SetAnchorPeer), check for existence first and replace the matching entry rather than appending a duplicate.",
    "Rename Base() to BaseConfig() and Updated() to UpdatedConfig() for clarity and consistency.",
    "For the chainable API (U3), introduce intermediary wrapper types: Application, Organization (Org), Orderer, Consortium, Channel, and Profile. Each wraps the appropriate ConfigGroup proto and exposes the relevant getters/setters.",
    "For U4 and U5, move ONLY types (not functions, except those deemed more appropriate per spec) into the new subpackages; the existing ConfigTx methods continue to live in the configtx package and reference the moved types via their new namespaces.",
    "For U6, remove the error return from removal functions since a non-existent removal is no longer an error condition; callers no longer need to handle the error.",
    "For U8, use the cobra CLI framework (the de-facto standard for Go CLIs) for command/subcommand/flag parsing, since the fabric project already uses it.",
    "Maintain backward compatibility at the public API level where possible (e.g., deprecating rather than removing) to minimize downstream impact on fabric core and other consumers.",
    "Execute the refactor as a single coherent increment so that intermediate broken states never reach main, requiring careful sequencing of file changes."
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    "The developer is fluent in Go and familiar with the existing Hyperledger Fabric configtx package structure, protobuf, and go.mod conventions.",
    "The existing configtx package contains roughly 20-30 AddXXX/UpdateXXX functions and 10-15 supporting types that need to be touched by the refactor.",
    "A meaningful unit test suite already exists for the configtx package and will be updated/extended rather than written from scratch.",
    "The fabric integration test suite (in test/fabric) also exercises the configtx package and will be run for verification.",
    "For the CLI, cobra + viper is acceptable; no exotic framework selection is required.",
    "Effort estimates assume focused, uninterrupted development time on a single developer's machine without context-switching, code reviews, or merge conflicts.",
    "No new third-party dependencies are introduced; everything uses libraries already present in fabric (cobra, viper, gopkg.in/yaml.v2, etc.).",
    "Backward-incompatible signature changes (e.g., removing the error return from remove functions) are acceptable per the spec's wording.",
    "Documentation effort covers inline GoDoc, the package-level README, and CHANGELOG entries only - no separate user-facing docs site updates."
  ],
  "activities": [
    {
      "activity_id": "A1",
      "activity_name": "Code analysis and design",
      "activity_description": "Read and understand the existing configtx package (types, functions, callers, tests). Design the new package layout (orderer/, membership/ subpackages), design the chainable intermediate types (Application, Organization, Orderer, Channel, Consortium), design the CLI command/flag structure, and record design decisions. Identify the full set of Add/Update functions to rename and the internal call sites that must change.",
      "most_likely_effort": 8
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A2",
      "activity_name": "U1: Rename Add/Update functions to Set with override semantics",
      "activity_description": "Rename all AddXXX and UpdateXXX functions in the configtx package to SetXXX. For map-backed config values (organizations, policies, ACLs, capabilities) implement pure override semantics. For slice-backed values (e.g., SetAnchorPeer) check for an existing matching entry and replace it in place rather than appending a duplicate. Update all example code, GoDoc, internal callers, and unit tests accordingly.",
      "most_likely_effort": 14
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A3",
      "activity_name": "U2: Rename Base() and Updated()",
      "activity_description": "Rename Base() to BaseConfig() and Updated() to UpdatedConfig() on the ConfigTx type. Update the function GoDocs to clearly describe what each returns. Update all internal callers in fabric core and the configtx package's own examples/tests.",
      "most_likely_effort": 3
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A4",
      "activity_name": "U3: Implement chainable API",
      "activity_description": "Introduce intermediate wrapper types (Application, Organization/Org, Orderer, Consortium, Channel, Profile) that wrap the relevant ConfigGroup proto. Move the existing Add/Set/Remove/Get methods onto the appropriate intermediate type so calls can be chained (e.g., c.Application().Org(orgName).SetAnchorPeer(...)). Update ConfigTx to expose the top-level intermediaries (Application(), Orderer(), Consortium(), Channel()). Refresh examples and GoDoc to demonstrate the chainable style, and update tests to cover the new API surface.",
      "most_likely_effort": 32
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A5",
      "activity_name": "U4: Create orderer subpackage",
      "activity_description": "Create a new orderer/ subpackage within configtx. Move the orderer-related types (BatchSize, Kafka, EtcdRaft, ConsensusState) into it. Decide whether orderer.Policies should be a new type or left as-is given shared policy machinery. Update all references throughout the package and in fabric callers. Add a well-formed package-level GoDoc for the new orderer package.",
      "most_likely_effort": 8
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A6",
      "activity_name": "U5: Create membership subpackage",
      "activity_description": "Create a new membership/ subpackage within configtx. Move the MSP-related types (SigningIdentityInfo, OUIdentifier, CryptoConfig, NodeOUs) into it. Update all references throughout the package and in fabric callers. Add a well-formed package-level GoDoc for the new membership package.",
      "most_likely_effort": 8
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A7",
      "activity_name": "U6: Make remove functions tolerant of missing values",
      "activity_description": "Modify all RemoveXXX functions so that removing a value that does not exist produces no error. Where it makes sense, drop the error return from the function signature entirely. Update GoDoc to clearly state that removal is a no-op when the value is absent. Update tests to cover the new behavior and remove now-unnecessary error assertions.",
      "most_likely_effort": 4
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A8",
      "activity_name": "U7: GoDoc cleanup and example restructuring",
      "activity_description": "Delete the eight unnecessary examples listed in the spec. Improve the GoDoc around configtx.New(). Rename Example_xxx functions to reflect their actual purpose (e.g., Example_systemChannel -> Example_textExample). Split examples into more focused functions (Example_ACLs, Example_policyRetrieval, Example_ordererUpdate, Example_membership, etc.). Strip // Output: validation from all examples. Add line breaks to code blocks so nothing requires horizontal scrolling in the generated GoDoc.",
      "most_likely_effort": 8
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A9",
      "activity_name": "U8: Build configtx CLI",
      "activity_description": "Set up a cobra-based CLI binary (e.g., cmd/configtx) that consumes the configtx library. Implement commands for: (a) generating a system channel genesis block, (b) generating an application channel creation transaction, and (c) producing config update transactions for all updatable config properties (e.g., addAnchorPeer, setMSP, removeOrg, etc.) via flags and subcommands. Wire it to load an input config, call into the refactored library, and emit a signed config update or genesis block. Write comprehensive help text and a usage manual.",
      "most_likely_effort": 32
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A10",
      "activity_name": "Unit testing",
      "activity_description": "Add or update unit tests for: the new Set functions (including slice vs map behavior), the chainable API, the orderer and membership subpackages, the modified remove functions, the renamed BaseConfig/UpdatedConfig accessors, and the CLI commands. Ensure coverage of both happy paths and edge cases (e.g., setting a value that already exists, removing a value that doesn't exist).",
      "most_likely_effort": 20
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A11",
      "activity_name": "Integration testing and verification",
      "activity_description": "Run the full fabric unit test suite plus the integration tests that exercise configtx (test/fabric scenarios such as channel creation, config updates, org addition/removal, anchor peer updates). Debug and fix any regressions introduced by the refactor. Smoke test the new CLI end-to-end against representative input configs.",
      "most_likely_effort": 8
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A12",
      "activity_name": "Documentation, cleanup, and finalization",
      "activity_description": "Update the package README and any relevant top-level docs to reflect the new API. Add CHANGELOG entries describing the breaking changes (Set prefix, BaseConfig/UpdatedConfig renames, chainable API, new subpackages, remove semantics). Run gofmt, golint, and go vet. Self-review the diff, squash commits, and prepare the change for submission.",
      "most_likely_effort": 4
    }
  ],
  "reported_total": {
    "most_likely_effort": 149
  }
}
```

